DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Creates a new storage pool containing the virtual devices specified on
     the command line.  The pool name must begin with a letter, and can only
     contain alphanumeric characters as well as the underscore ("__"), dash
     ("--"), colon ("::"), space (" "), and period ("..").  The pool names
     mmiirrrroorr, rraaiiddzz, ddrraaiidd, ssppaarree and lloogg are reserved, as are names beginning
     with mmiirrrroorr, rraaiiddzz, ddrraaiidd, and ssppaarree.  The _v_d_e_v specification is de‐
     scribed in the _V_i_r_t_u_a_l _D_e_v_i_c_e_s section of zpoolconcepts(7).

     The command attempts to verify that each device specified is accessible
     and not currently in use by another subsystem.  However this check is not
     robust enough to detect simultaneous attempts to use a new device in dif‐
     ferent pools, even if mmuullttiihhoosstt= eennaabblleedd.  The administrator must ensure
     that simultaneous invocations of any combination of zzppooooll rreeppllaaccee, zzppooooll
     ccrreeaattee, zzppooooll aadddd, or zzppooooll llaabbeellcclleeaarr do not refer to the same device.
     Using the same device in two pools will result in pool corruption.

     There are some uses, such as being currently mounted, or specified as the
     dedicated dump device, that prevents a device from ever being used by
     ZFS.  Other uses, such as having a preexisting UFS file system, can be
     overridden with --ff.

     The command also checks that the replication strategy for the pool is
     consistent.  An attempt to combine redundant and non-redundant storage in
     a single pool, or to mix disks and files, results in an error unless --ff
     is specified.  The use of differently-sized devices within a single raidz
     or mirror group is also flagged as an error unless --ff is specified.

     Unless the --RR option is specified, the default mount point is _/_p_o_o_l.  The
     mount point must not exist or must be empty, or else the root dataset
     will not be able to be be mounted.  This can be overridden with the --mm
     option.

     By default all supported features are enabled on the new pool.  The --dd
     option and the --oo _c_o_m_p_a_t_i_b_i_l_i_t_y property (e.g --oo ccoommppaattiibbiilliittyy=_2_0_2_0) can
     be used to restrict the features that are enabled, so that the pool can
     be imported on other releases of ZFS.

     --dd        Do not enable any features on the new pool.  Individual fea‐
               tures can be enabled by setting their corresponding properties
